1
00:00:11,950 --> 00:00:15,950
This video provides guidance for the safe use of centrifuges.

2
00:00:15,950 --> 00:00:21,950
It is intended to supplement the formal instruction provided in each institution and each laboratory.

3
00:00:21,950 --> 00:00:25,950
This video is not a substitute for a safety training program.

4
00:00:25,950 --> 00:00:35,970
What does it look like when a centrifuge rotor fails?

5
00:00:35,970 --> 00:00:39,470
Not a pretty picture.

6
00:00:39,470 --> 00:00:44,470
Although personal injury is rare, damage to one's budget can be severe.

7
00:00:44,470 --> 00:00:54,160
Centrifuges are an important tool in the molecular biology lab, and they deserve to be treated with respect.

8
00:00:54,160 --> 00:01:11,640
Ninety percent of centrifuge-related failures are user errors.

9
00:01:11,640 --> 00:01:15,640
Careless centrifugation can mean lost samples or damaged equipment.

10
00:01:15,640 --> 00:01:22,230
It can also present a risk to the user and the lab.

11
00:01:22,230 --> 00:01:33,180
Every time one uses a centrifuge, one makes a series of choices.

12
00:01:33,180 --> 00:01:39,180
Which centrifuge, which rotor, which tubes and adapters, what speed, for how long?

13
00:01:39,180 --> 00:01:44,180
These choices can be critical for both effectiveness and safety.

14
00:01:44,180 --> 00:01:49,330
Centrifuges are designed to use certain rotors.

15
00:01:49,330 --> 00:01:55,330
Rotors are designed to be run up to a maximum speed with a load of a specific weight.

16
00:01:55,330 --> 00:01:59,330
Exceeding those limits invites trouble.

17
00:01:59,330 --> 00:02:04,330
If you are unsure of the rotor or proper tube size, consult the manual.

18
00:02:04,330 --> 00:02:14,330
Ask an experienced colleague or call the manufacturer's representative.

19
00:02:14,330 --> 00:02:22,930
It's easy to take tubes for granted, but the correct fit of a tube in a rotor is crucial.

20
00:02:22,930 --> 00:02:28,930
Putting 600,000 Gs on anything that's not a perfect fit is going to lead to grief.

21
00:02:28,930 --> 00:02:36,540
There's another important choice one makes when centrifuging.

22
00:02:36,540 --> 00:02:40,540
What level of containment is necessary?

23
00:02:40,540 --> 00:02:46,540
Always choose aerosol containment tubes and rotors when centrifuging infectious materials

24
00:02:46,540 --> 00:02:53,680
and load and unload in a biological safety cabinet.

25
00:02:53,680 --> 00:03:02,500
Even when working with non-hazardous materials,

26
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:07,500
every step is critical in preparing a centrifuge run.

27
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:14,500
Before placing the rotor in the centrifuge, make sure the bowl is dry and the drive spindle is clean.

28
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:21,160
Avoid overfilling of tubes or bottles.

29
00:03:21,160 --> 00:03:25,160
Remember, in tubes used in fixed angle rotors,

30
00:03:25,160 --> 00:03:34,220
centrifugal force drives the fluid up the outside tube wall.

31
00:03:34,220 --> 00:03:38,220
Check to make sure the rotor is seated on the drive hub correctly.

32
00:03:38,220 --> 00:03:42,370
Balancing is critical.

33
00:03:42,370 --> 00:03:50,370
Half a gram difference is nothing at 1G, but at 500,000 Gs, it's 250 kilograms.

34
00:03:50,370 --> 00:03:54,030
And that's another story.

35
00:03:54,030 --> 00:04:02,530
Don't overload beyond the rotor's maximum mass without reducing the rated rotor speed.

36
00:04:02,530 --> 00:04:08,530
When using a horizontal rotor, make sure all buckets are hooked correctly and move freely.

37
00:04:08,530 --> 00:04:16,090
Check O-rings on containers and rotor for cracks, nicks, or chemical attack.

38
00:04:16,090 --> 00:04:23,490
And don't forget to apply vacuum grease to the seals at least weekly.

39
00:04:23,490 --> 00:04:28,490
The most common user errors are failure to secure the rotor to the drive,

40
00:04:28,490 --> 00:04:31,490
to put the lid on the rotor, and to secure it.

41
00:04:31,490 --> 00:04:39,080
Double check to make sure the rotor is not being run beyond its rated maximum.

42
00:04:39,080 --> 00:04:57,460
Yes, it may be a boring task, but it's good practice to stay at the centrifuge

43
00:04:57,460 --> 00:05:03,180
until it's running smoothly at the desired run speed.

44
00:05:03,180 --> 00:05:08,180
When the run is completed, don't open the door until the rotor has come to a halt.

45
00:05:08,180 --> 00:05:16,020
Always check for a possible spill, and if you find one,

46
00:05:16,020 --> 00:05:20,020
be sure to clean both centrifuge and rotor thoroughly.

47
00:05:20,020 --> 00:05:29,120
When working with infectious materials, wait 10 minutes before opening the door

48
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:33,370
in order to avoid hazardous aerosols.

49
00:05:33,370 --> 00:05:36,370
If there's evidence of leakage or damage of any kind,

50
00:05:36,370 --> 00:05:40,370
close the lid immediately and carefully plan the cleanup.

51
00:05:40,370 --> 00:05:49,300
What all users of centrifuges fear most is rotor failure.

52
00:05:49,300 --> 00:05:53,300
Centrifuges and rotors are designed to withstand enormous forces,

53
00:05:53,300 --> 00:06:01,220
but the integrity of rotors can be seriously compromised by corrosion or fatigue.

54
00:06:01,220 --> 00:06:06,220
In aluminum rotors, structural failure is most often caused by surface corrosion

55
00:06:06,220 --> 00:06:09,220
in the highly stressed areas of the rotor.

56
00:06:09,220 --> 00:06:14,220
Metal fatigue is an inevitable consequence of prolonged rotor use

57
00:06:14,220 --> 00:06:17,220
and will eventually cause any rotor to fail.

58
00:06:17,220 --> 00:06:26,140
Rotor failure doesn't have to happen if rotors are properly maintained and retired.

59
00:06:26,140 --> 00:06:29,140
Follow the manufacturer's instructions.

60
00:06:29,140 --> 00:06:31,140
Here are some general guidelines.

61
00:06:31,140 --> 00:06:38,850
Keeping the equipment clean and dry is critical.

62
00:06:38,850 --> 00:06:44,850
Wash immediately if spillage occurs or if salts or other corrosive materials have been used.

63
00:06:44,850 --> 00:06:53,340
Avoid harsh detergents or bottle brushes with sharp wire ends,

64
00:06:53,340 --> 00:06:57,340
especially when cleaning aluminum rotors, which are more susceptible to corrosion.

65
00:06:57,340 --> 00:07:09,970
Finish rinsing with deionized water.

66
00:07:09,970 --> 00:07:16,520
Make a habit of inspecting rotors.

67
00:07:16,520 --> 00:07:21,520
If there are rough spots, pitting, white powder deposits, or heavy discoloration,

68
00:07:21,520 --> 00:07:23,520
don't run the rotor.

69
00:07:23,520 --> 00:07:26,520
Have it checked by the manufacturer's representative.

70
00:07:26,520 --> 00:07:32,570
Eventually, every rotor must be retired.

71
00:07:32,570 --> 00:07:37,570
And as ultra rotors age, their maximum speeds must be derated.

72
00:07:37,570 --> 00:07:41,570
That's why it's imperative to keep diligent logs.

73
00:07:41,570 --> 00:07:45,570
An unlocked ultra-speed rotor is a ticking time bomb.

74
00:07:45,570 --> 00:07:50,780
Centrifugation isn't as simple as it appears.

75
00:07:50,780 --> 00:07:55,780
It requires careful use, careful maintenance, and careful bookkeeping.

76
00:07:55,780 --> 00:08:02,570
All the information needed is in the owner manuals or a phone call away.

77
00:08:02,570 --> 00:08:05,880
Find out.

78
00:08:05,880 --> 00:08:08,880
Don't let carelessness destroy your centrifuge.

79
00:08:08,880 --> 00:08:13,880
It may ruin your experiment, not to mention your day.
